From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] EDEN_UP_FAST_CFG=1: ordered CFG block lookup and two-sided sibling walk at shader translation, and a pipeline a draw waits for goes to the front of the builder queue; off by default

Port of EdGlantine-PS5 24c41c41aa (UB-056, 99zzzz4-faster-shader-translation.diff), itself from
Lichii #71. Two parts:

- Translation: CFG::InspectVisitedBlocks finds the block that holds pc with an ordered lookup
  instead of a scan of every block, and AreOrdered walks from both siblings at once. These are the
  bodies 0172 already carries under EDEN_NX2_SHADER_FAST_SCAN; EDEN_UP_FAST_CFG=1 turns on the
  same path (one static per function reads both names), no second copy of the code.
- Builds: upstream lets the first worker or waiter claim a pipeline build, so a draw that waits
  does not sit behind hundreds of queued builds. Here the waiter never builds. The pipeline holds
  its build task; the constructor queues a claim-once copy, and a draw or dispatch that finds the
  pipeline unbuilt asks the builder once more with ThreadWorker::QueueWorkFront, ahead of the
  builds QueueWork queued but behind earlier front entries, so a burst P1..PN is built P1 first,
  the pipeline the first wait is for (BuildFirst, on the GPU thread, before the wait is recorded;
  the task and mesh-as-compute path asks the same way before its own wait). Whichever copy a
  VkPipelineBuilder thread takes first builds; the other returns. The build thus stays on a
  VkPipelineBuilder thread, the only threads the heap lets wait for memory (EDEN_UP_HEAP_WAIT_MS,
  headless/heap_arenas.inc):
  the upstream form would run RADV's compiler on the VulkanWorker scheduler thread, where a NULL
  from the pool ends the session in nir_opt_copy_prop_vars and the wait would hold
  execution_mutex. The Vulkan failure path (is_built published, MarkShaderComplete) is as before;
  any other exception publishes completion, drops the task and rethrows, as upstream.
  With more than one builder thread the front entry goes to the next free one; all busy, the
  draw waits for the first to finish, as now.

Unset: the constructor queues the task as before, the waits are unchanged, the CFG reads only
EDEN_NX2_SHADER_FAST_SCAN. ThreadWorker's queue is a std::deque with the same push-back/pop-front
order (std::queue was that deque); its front_queued count stays 0, so a pop does what it did. No
cache key, serialization or output change.

Not ported: upstream's foreign host pointer guard in src/core (memory.cpp, device_memory_manager.h);
a separate change, not needed for either part above.

Checked: git apply --check on a copy of the T88 core with patches through 0493. Not compiled, not
run on the console.

